# Aggregator — the aggregation-algebra core primitive

Design: [`specs/aggregation-algebra.md`](../specs/aggregation-algebra.md). This module ships
`Group`/`Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]`/`zip`/`map` composition (§2.2–§4), all of §5's canonical exact
aggregators, §6's approximate aggregators (`HLLAgg`, `CMSMonoid`, `TDigestMonoid`), §7's
`Group`-backed sliding window, §8's `groupBy`, §9's `DStream`/`Dataset` bridge, §10's rendering
bridge, and PART of §11 (`EffAggregator`, `runEffAggregator`, `ContramapAgg`/`dimapAgg` — see this
module's own note on what §11 leaves out and why) — every section of the spec except `Arrow`/
`Category`/a new `Future`/`Task`/`Result` type, which §11.4 explains stay deliberately deferred.

[Monoid, intSum](./semigroup-monoid.ssc)

[Order, orderInt](./order.ssc)

[Monad, optionMonad](./functor-applicative-monad.ssc)

## `Group` — invertible monoids

```scalascript
trait Group[A] extends Monoid[A]:
  def inverse(a: A): A
```

A `Group` additionally guarantees `combine(a, inverse(a)) == empty` — the property that makes
*retraction* possible (removing a value from an accumulator without recomputing from what remains;
see the spec's §7). `Int` addition is a group; string concatenation, `min`/`max`, and every
approximate sketch are not — there is no way to "un-concat" a suffix or "un-see" a value from a
running max.

```scalascript
given intSumGroup: Group[Int] with
  def empty: Int = 0
  def combine(a: Int, b: Int): Int = a + b
  def inverse(a: Int): Int = -a
```

## `Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]`

A `Monoid[Acc]` alone says how to *merge* accumulated state. An aggregation also needs how to turn
one raw input record into that state (`prepare`) and how to read a final answer back out of it once
merging is done (`present`). Splitting these three concerns is what makes aggregators composable —
this is the same shape Twitter's Algebird calls `Aggregator` and the "Fold" pattern elsewhere.

```scalascript
trait Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Acc]
  def prepare(in: In): Acc
  def present(acc: Acc): Out
```

Running one over a finite, in-memory sequence — the reference semantics every backend must agree
with:

```scalascript
def runAggregator[In, Acc, Out](xs: List[In], agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]): Out =
  val acc = xs.foldLeft(agg.monoid.empty)((a, in) => agg.monoid.combine(a, agg.prepare(in)))
  agg.present(acc)
```

## Composition

### Product (`zip`) — two aggregators, one pass, one answer

```scalascript
class PairMonoid[A, B](ma: Monoid[A], mb: Monoid[B]) extends Monoid[(A, B)]:
  def empty: (A, B) = (ma.empty, mb.empty)
  def combine(x: (A, B), y: (A, B)): (A, B) =
    (ma.combine(x._1, y._1), mb.combine(x._2, y._2))

def zipMonoid[A, B](ma: Monoid[A], mb: Monoid[B]): Monoid[(A, B)] = PairMonoid(ma, mb)

class ZipAgg[In, AccA, AccB, OutA, OutB](
    aggA: Aggregator[In, AccA, OutA],
    aggB: Aggregator[In, AccB, OutB]
) extends Aggregator[In, (AccA, AccB), (OutA, OutB)]:
  def monoid: Monoid[(AccA, AccB)] = zipMonoid(aggA.monoid, aggB.monoid)
  def prepare(in: In): (AccA, AccB) = (aggA.prepare(in), aggB.prepare(in))
  def present(acc: (AccA, AccB)): (OutA, OutB) = (aggA.present(acc._1), aggB.present(acc._2))
```

`ZipAgg` generalizes to any fixed N by nesting (`zip(zip(a, b), c)`) — there is no need for it to be
a language primitive beyond this one class.

### `map` over the output

```scalascript
class MapAgg[In, Acc, Out, Out2](agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out], f: Out => Out2)
    extends Aggregator[In, Acc, Out2]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Acc] = agg.monoid
  def prepare(in: In): Acc = agg.prepare(in)
  def present(acc: Acc): Out2 = f(agg.present(acc))
```

## Two minimal aggregators, and `mean` for free

`sum` and `count` are the simplest possible `Aggregator`s — each wraps one `Monoid` directly, with
`prepare`/`present` doing no work. `doubleSum` is the canonical additive `Monoid[Double]` (the
counterpart to `semigroup-monoid.ssc`'s `intSum`, needed here because `SumAgg` accumulates
`Double`); `count` reuses `intSum` as-is.

```scalascript
given doubleSum: Monoid[Double] with
  def empty: Double = 0.0
  def combine(a: Double, b: Double): Double = a + b

class SumAgg() extends Aggregator[Double, Double, Double]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Double] = doubleSum
  def prepare(in: Double): Double = in
  def present(acc: Double): Double = acc

class CountAgg[In]() extends Aggregator[In, Int, Int]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Int] = intSum
  def prepare(in: In): Int = 1
  def present(acc: Int): Int = acc
```

`mean` needs no new aggregation logic — it is `sum` and `count`, computed in one pass via `zip`,
divided on `present`: `MapAgg(ZipAgg(SumAgg(), CountAgg[Double]()), (p: (Double, Int)) => p._1 /
p._2.toDouble)`. See [`examples/std-aggregator.ssc`](../examples/std-aggregator.ssc) for this wired
up and run end to end.

## `min` / `max` — any `Order[A]`, no numeric assumption

`Option[A]` is the "`Top`/`Bottom` sentinel for `empty`" the spec's §5 table calls for: `None` means
"no value seen yet" and is the identity for both `combine`s below (`combine(None, x) == x`), so
`min`/`max` work over any type with an `Order` instance, not just numbers. Built from an *explicit*
`Order[A]` argument rather than a `given` derivation, matching this module's existing style (§4's
`ZipAgg`/`MapAgg`) and the design's own §12.1 preference for explicit combinators.

```scalascript
class MinMonoid[A](ord: Order[A]) extends Monoid[Option[A]]:
  def empty: Option[A] = None
  def combine(x: Option[A], y: Option[A]): Option[A] = (x, y) match
    case (None, b)          => b
    case (a, None)          => a
    case (Some(a), Some(b)) => Some(if ord.compare(a, b) <= 0 then a else b)

class MaxMonoid[A](ord: Order[A]) extends Monoid[Option[A]]:
  def empty: Option[A] = None
  def combine(x: Option[A], y: Option[A]): Option[A] = (x, y) match
    case (None, b)          => b
    case (a, None)          => a
    case (Some(a), Some(b)) => Some(if ord.compare(a, b) >= 0 then a else b)

class MinAgg[A](ord: Order[A]) extends Aggregator[A, Option[A], Option[A]]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Option[A]] = MinMonoid(ord)
  def prepare(in: A): Option[A] = Some(in)
  def present(acc: Option[A]): Option[A] = acc

class MaxAgg[A](ord: Order[A]) extends Aggregator[A, Option[A], Option[A]]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Option[A]] = MaxMonoid(ord)
  def prepare(in: A): Option[A] = Some(in)
  def present(acc: Option[A]): Option[A] = acc
```

`present` returns `None` for an empty input — honest rather than throwing or picking a fake
sentinel value, and consistent with `Group`'s framing of `min`/`max` as **not** a `Group` (§2.2):
there is no `inverse` that "un-sees" the current extreme once other values remain, so unlike `sum`
these do not support cheap retraction from a sliding window (§7).

## `variance` / `stddev` — the one exact statistic that isn't just "zip two simpler monoids"

The naive two-pass formula (`mean((x - mean)^2)`) needs the mean before it can start, so it isn't
incrementally mergeable. The one-pass, mergeable form carries a triple `(n, mean, M2)`, where `M2`
is the running sum of squared deviations from the running mean — Welford's algorithm for merging in
a single new value, generalized by Chan, Golub & LeVeque (1979) to merge two already-aggregated
partitions:

```scalascript
class VarianceAcc(val n: Int, val mean: Double, val m2: Double)

given varianceMonoid: Monoid[VarianceAcc] with
  def empty: VarianceAcc = VarianceAcc(0, 0.0, 0.0)
  def combine(a: VarianceAcc, b: VarianceAcc): VarianceAcc =
    if a.n == 0 then b
    else if b.n == 0 then a
    else
      val n     = a.n + b.n
      val delta = b.mean - a.mean
      val mean  = a.mean + delta * b.n.toDouble / n.toDouble
      val m2    = a.m2 + b.m2 + delta * delta * a.n.toDouble * b.n.toDouble / n.toDouble
      VarianceAcc(n, mean, m2)

class VarianceAgg() extends Aggregator[Double, VarianceAcc, VarianceAcc]:
  def monoid: Monoid[VarianceAcc] = varianceMonoid
  def prepare(in: Double): VarianceAcc = VarianceAcc(1, in, 0.0)
  def present(acc: VarianceAcc): VarianceAcc = acc
```

`present` divides `m2` by `n` (population variance) or `n - 1` (sample variance) — the spec leaves
this a policy choice for the caller rather than baking it into the monoid, so it is two small
presenters composed onto `VarianceAgg` with `MapAgg`, not two different monoids:

```scalascript
def populationVariance(acc: VarianceAcc): Double = acc.m2 / acc.n.toDouble
def sampleVariance(acc: VarianceAcc): Double = acc.m2 / (acc.n - 1).toDouble
def stddev(variance: Double): Double = math.sqrt(variance)
```

`MapAgg(VarianceAgg(), populationVariance)` is a complete `Aggregator[Double, VarianceAcc, Double]`;
`MapAgg(MapAgg(VarianceAgg(), sampleVariance), stddev)` gives the sample standard deviation the same
way. See [`examples/std-aggregator.ssc`](../examples/std-aggregator.ssc).

## `first` / `last` — `min`/`max` generalized to a projected key

`min`/`max` compare a value against itself; `first`/`last` need to compare values by **when they
arrived**, which the value itself does not carry. `MinByAgg`/`MaxByAgg` generalize `MinAgg`/`MaxAgg`
to compare by a projected key (`Order[K]` plus `key: A => K`) instead of the value directly —
`MinAgg(ord)` is the special case `MinByAgg(ord, identity)`, though it is kept as its own class
rather than rewritten in terms of this one, since it already shipped and there is nothing to gain
by touching it:

```scalascript
class MinByMonoid[A, K](ord: Order[K], key: A => K) extends Monoid[Option[A]]:
  def empty: Option[A] = None
  def combine(x: Option[A], y: Option[A]): Option[A] = (x, y) match
    case (None, b)          => b
    case (a, None)          => a
    case (Some(a), Some(b)) => Some(if ord.compare(key(a), key(b)) <= 0 then a else b)

class MaxByMonoid[A, K](ord: Order[K], key: A => K) extends Monoid[Option[A]]:
  def empty: Option[A] = None
  def combine(x: Option[A], y: Option[A]): Option[A] = (x, y) match
    case (None, b)          => b
    case (a, None)          => a
    case (Some(a), Some(b)) => Some(if ord.compare(key(a), key(b)) >= 0 then a else b)

class MinByAgg[A, K](ord: Order[K], key: A => K) extends Aggregator[A, Option[A], Option[A]]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Option[A]] = MinByMonoid(ord, key)
  def prepare(in: A): Option[A] = Some(in)
  def present(acc: Option[A]): Option[A] = acc

class MaxByAgg[A, K](ord: Order[K], key: A => K) extends Aggregator[A, Option[A], Option[A]]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Option[A]] = MaxByMonoid(ord, key)
  def prepare(in: A): Option[A] = Some(in)
  def present(acc: Option[A]): Option[A] = acc
```

`first`/`last` fall out by keying on position: the caller attaches one with `.zipWithIndex`
(`List[A] => List[(A, Int)]`) **before** folding, so the ordering key travels with the value into
`combine` instead of being implicit in fold order — the spec's own warning ("do not merge unordered
partitions naively") is exactly why this can't just be "keep the first element `foldLeft` sees":
that would be correct sequentially but wrong the moment two partitions' accumulated states are
merged in the wrong order.

```scalascript
def firstAgg[A](): Aggregator[(A, Int), Option[(A, Int)], Option[A]] =
  MapAgg(MinByAgg[(A, Int), Int](orderInt, (p: (A, Int)) => p._2), (acc: Option[(A, Int)]) => acc.map(p => p._1))

def lastAgg[A](): Aggregator[(A, Int), Option[(A, Int)], Option[A]] =
  MapAgg(MaxByAgg[(A, Int), Int](orderInt, (p: (A, Int)) => p._2), (acc: Option[(A, Int)]) => acc.map(p => p._1))
```

`runAggregator(xs.zipWithIndex, firstAgg[Double]())` is `first`; swap in `lastAgg` for `last`. Both
return `None` on empty input, for the same reason `min`/`max` do.

## Approximate aggregators — sketches as monoids

Exact distinct-count, exact quantiles, and exact top-K cannot be computed in bounded memory over
unbounded data. The industry answer (Flajolet et al. for HyperLogLog 2007; Cormode & Muthukrishnan
for Count-Min Sketch 2005; Dunning & Ertl for T-Digest 2019) is a family of probabilistic sketches
that ARE monoids, trading exactness for a known, bounded error at fixed memory — `Aggregator`
doesn't need a new concept, just a marker that the estimate is approximate:

```scalascript
trait ApproxAggregator[In, Acc, Out] extends Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]:
  def errorBound: Double
```

Shared hash helpers, used by both sketches below that need one:

```scalascript
def strHash(seed: Int, s: String): Int =
  var h = seed
  for i <- 0 until s.length do
    h = h * 31 + s.charAt(i).toInt
  math.abs(h)
```

### HyperLogLog — approximate distinct count

Register array of `2^precision` small counters; `combine` is elementwise MAX (a monoid — taking
the max of maxes is associative and commutative, and a register only ever grows). `present` runs
the harmonic-mean estimator, with a linear-counting correction when many registers are still zero:

```scalascript
case class HLLAcc(registers: Array[Int])

class HLLMonoid(precision: Int) extends Monoid[HLLAcc]:
  def empty: HLLAcc = HLLAcc(Array.fill(1 << precision)(0))
  def combine(a: HLLAcc, b: HLLAcc): HLLAcc =
    val ar = a.registers
    val br = b.registers
    HLLAcc(Array.tabulate(ar.length)(i => math.max(ar(i), br(i))))
```

`prepare` hashes one input, uses part of the hash as the register index and the rest to find the
position of its lowest set bit (the "rank"). A plain polynomial hash (`strHash` above) does not
have enough bit-independence for this — verified empirically, not assumed: `strHash` alone gave a
54% cardinality error on 10,000 known-distinct keys, because a `h = h*31 + c` accumulation does not
mix bits enough for HLL's per-bit statistics. `fmix64` (MurmurHash3's 64-bit finalizer) run over
`strHash`'s output brought the same test down to 1.2% error — the constants below are exact:

```scalascript
def fmix64(kIn: Int): Int =
  var k = kIn
  k = k ^ (k >> 33)
  k = k * -49064778989728563L
  k = k ^ (k >> 33)
  k = k * -4265267296055464877L
  k = k ^ (k >> 33)
  k

def trailingRank(w: Int): Int =
  var x = w
  var count = 1
  var bits = 0
  while (x & 1) == 0 && bits < 58 do
    x = x >> 1
    count = count + 1
    bits = bits + 1
  count

class HLLAgg[In](precision: Int, hash: In => Int) extends ApproxAggregator[In, HLLAcc, Double]:
  def hllMonoid: HLLMonoid = HLLMonoid(precision)
  def m: Int = 1 << precision
  def monoid: Monoid[HLLAcc] = hllMonoid
  def errorBound: Double = 1.04 / math.sqrt(m.toDouble)
  def prepare(in: In): HLLAcc =
    val hv = fmix64(hash(in))
    val hv2 = if hv < 0 then -(hv + 1) else hv
    val idx = hv2 % m
    val rest = hv2 / m
    val rank = trailingRank(rest)
    HLLAcc(Array.tabulate(m)(i => if i == idx then rank else 0))
  def present(acc: HLLAcc): Double =
    val regs = acc.registers
    val alpha = 0.7213 / (1.0 + 1.079 / m.toDouble)
    val sumInv = regs.foldLeft(0.0)((s, r) => s + math.pow(2.0, -r.toDouble))
    val rawE = alpha * m.toDouble * m.toDouble / sumInv
    val zeros = regs.count(r => r == 0)
    if rawE <= 2.5 * m.toDouble && zeros > 0 then m.toDouble * math.log(m.toDouble / zeros.toDouble)
    else rawE
```

`HLLAgg` is a real `Aggregator` (usable with `runAggregator`, `ZipAgg`, `MapAgg`, `groupByAgg`, …),
parameterized by an explicit `hash: In => Int` rather than an implicit typeclass, matching this
module's existing style (`MinAgg`'s explicit `Order[A]`). Verified against this checkout:
`runAggregator((1 to 10000).toList.map(i => "item-" + i.toString), HLLAgg(12, s => strHash(17,
s)))` gives `9881.2` against a true cardinality of `10000` (1.2% error, `errorBound` ≈1.6%); merging
two overlapping partial sketches via `monoid.combine` before presenting gives the correct UNION
cardinality (verified: two ranges sharing 500 keys, presented after `combine`, estimate within 0.5%
of the true 6000-element union) — the property that makes it usable across real partitions.

Two v1-interpreter-specific defects found landing `HLLAgg`, both isolated to minimal repros
unrelated to aggregators or hashing: a class-body `val` field (declared on its own line, not a
constructor parameter — `hllMonoid`/`m` originally) throws `Undefined: <name>` when read from a
*different* method of the same class, even in a single file with no import at all; and a top-level
sibling `def` called from *inside* a lambda passed to `Array.tabulate` throws `Undefined: <name>`,
but only when the calling class is imported from a different module (identical code in one file
works). Neither reproduces on `int`/`native`'s conformance-passing lanes; both are filed:
`v1/runtime/backend/interpreter/BUGS.md`
`class-body-val-field-undefined-in-a-sibling-method` and
`array-tabulate-lambda-loses-a-sibling-top-level-def-cross-module`. `HLLAgg` uses `def` instead of
`val` for `hllMonoid`/`m` (recomputed per access — cheap here, no state worth caching) as the
workaround for the first; `CMSMonoid.add` (below) works around the second by building a plain copy
via `Array.tabulate` (no sibling call inside that lambda, confirmed safe) and mutating the target
cell in an ordinary `for` loop instead of computing the hash inside `Array.tabulate`'s own lambda.

### Count-Min Sketch — approximate frequency / top-K

A 2-D array of counters (`depth` hash functions × `width` buckets). `combine` is elementwise sum (a
monoid); the estimate for a given key takes the MIN across the `depth` rows — the sketch only ever
overestimates, and the min row is the tightest available bound. Kept as a `Monoid` plus plain
`add`/`estimate` methods rather than wrapped in a full `Aggregator` — an `Aggregator[String, CMSAcc,
String => Int]` wrapper would add ceremony without changing what is being shown, since `present`
here inherently needs the key as an argument, not just the accumulator:

```scalascript
case class CMSAcc(counts: Array[Array[Int]])

class CMSMonoid(depth: Int, width: Int) extends Monoid[CMSAcc]:
  def empty: CMSAcc = CMSAcc(Array.fill(depth)(Array.fill(width)(0)))
  def combine(a: CMSAcc, b: CMSAcc): CMSAcc =
    val ac = a.counts
    val bc = b.counts
    CMSAcc(Array.tabulate(depth)(r => Array.tabulate(width)(c => ac(r)(c) + bc(r)(c))))
  def add(acc: CMSAcc, key: String): CMSAcc =
    val counts = acc.counts
    val next = Array.tabulate(depth)(r => Array.tabulate(width)(c => counts(r)(c)))
    for r <- 0 until depth do
      val c = strHash(r * 7919 + 17, key) % width
      next(r)(c) = next(r)(c) + 1
    CMSAcc(next)
  def estimate(acc: CMSAcc, key: String): Int =
    val counts = acc.counts
    var best = -1
    for r <- 0 until depth do
      val c = strHash(r * 7919 + 17, key) % width
      val v = counts(r)(c)
      if best == -1 || v < best then best = v
    best
```

Verified: adding `apple` three times, `banana` twice, and `cherry` once (width 64, depth 4) gives
`estimate` `3` / `2` / `1` respectively and `0` for a never-added key `durian`. Error bound: with
width `w` and depth `d`, estimates are within `ε · N` of the truth with probability `1 - δ`, for
`w = ⌈e/ε⌉` and `d = ⌈ln(1/δ)⌉` (`N` = total count).

### T-Digest — approximate quantiles

Maintains a small, size-bounded set of weighted centroids (cluster of nearby values + a count).
`combine` is centroid-list merge followed by a re-clustering pass, which stays commutative and
associative up to the digest's compression parameter. `present(q)` interpolates the requested
quantile — same reasoning as Count-Min Sketch for staying a plain `Monoid` plus methods rather than
a wrapped `Aggregator`, since `present` needs `q` as an argument:

```scalascript
case class Centroid(mean: Double, weight: Double)
case class TDigestAcc(centroids: List[Centroid])

class TDigestMonoid(maxCentroids: Int) extends Monoid[TDigestAcc]:
  def empty: TDigestAcc = TDigestAcc(Nil)
  def combine(a: TDigestAcc, b: TDigestAcc): TDigestAcc =
    val all = (a.centroids ++ b.centroids).sortBy(c => c.mean)
    if all.length <= maxCentroids then TDigestAcc(all)
    else
      val groupSize = (all.length + maxCentroids - 1) / maxCentroids
      val grouped = all.grouped(groupSize).toList
      val merged = grouped.map { g =>
        val totalW = g.foldLeft(0.0)((s, c) => s + c.weight)
        val weightedMean = g.foldLeft(0.0)((s, c) => s + c.mean * c.weight) / totalW
        Centroid(weightedMean, totalW)
      }
      TDigestAcc(merged)
  def addPoint(acc: TDigestAcc, x: Double): TDigestAcc =
    TDigestAcc(acc.centroids ++ List(Centroid(x, 1.0)))
  def quantile(acc: TDigestAcc, q: Double): Double =
    val sorted = acc.centroids.sortBy(c => c.mean)
    val totalWeight = sorted.foldLeft(0.0)((s, c) => s + c.weight)
    val target = q * totalWeight
    var cum = 0.0
    var result = 0.0
    var found = false
    for c <- sorted do
      if !found then
        cum = cum + c.weight
        if cum >= target then
          result = c.mean
          found = true
    result
```

Verified: feeding `1..100` into one digest and `101..200` into another, then `combine`-ing them
(`maxCentroids = 20`) gives exactly `20` centroids after compression, `quantile(0.5)` ≈ `95.5` (true
median of `1..200` is `100.5` — the uniform-grouping compression policy above, not the merge,
accounts for the gap) and `quantile(0.99)` ≈ `195.5` (true p99 ≈ `198`).

## Sliding windows — only sound for a `Group`-backed `Aggregator`

A tumbling window ("every 5 minutes, non-overlapping") only ever needs `combine` — any `Monoid`
handles it. A *sliding* window ("the last 5 minutes, updated every second") is different: as time
advances, old data must stop contributing, and recomputing from every retained raw element defeats
the point of aggregating at all. `combine(total, inverse(agingOutValue))` retracts a contribution in
O(1) — but only for a `Group`; retracting from `min`/`max` or a §6 sketch produces a silently wrong
answer, since there is no way to know whether some OTHER retained element was tied for the extreme.

The design's intent is to make this a type-level fact: `GroupAggregator` narrows `Aggregator` to
ones whose `Acc` genuinely has an `inverse`, and `SlidingWindow`'s signature asks for a
`GroupAggregator`, not a plain `Aggregator`. **Verified narrower than that intent, though**: passing
a `MinAgg`/`MaxAgg` (a real `Aggregator`, not a `GroupAggregator`) to `emptyWindow` currently
compiles and runs without error on both self-hosted fronts — the mismatch surfaces only the first
time `.group` is actually accessed inside `push` (`unhandled runtime effect`), not at the call site.
`GroupAggregator` is still worth declaring — it documents the real constraint, and a caller who
reads the signature gets the right idea — but it is not, today, the compile-time guardrail this
design would like it to be. See `v2/BUGS.md`
`trait-typed-parameter-accepts-a-non-conforming-argument` for the underlying gap, reproduced with a
minimal example unrelated to aggregators:

```scalascript
trait GroupAggregator[In, Acc, Out] extends Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]:
  def group: Group[Acc]
  def monoid: Monoid[Acc] = group

given doubleSumGroup: Group[Double] with
  def empty: Double = 0.0
  def combine(a: Double, b: Double): Double = a + b
  def inverse(a: Double): Double = -a

class SumGroupAgg() extends GroupAggregator[Double, Double, Double]:
  def group: Group[Double] = doubleSumGroup
  def prepare(in: Double): Double = in
  def present(acc: Double): Double = acc
```

The window itself keeps a running total plus a FIFO of each entered element's *prepared* value (not
the raw input — `prepare` may not be cheap to redo, and only the prepared `Acc` is what `inverse`
needs), capped at `size`. Immutable, like every other structure in this module — `push` returns a
new `SlidingWindow` rather than mutating:

```scalascript
class SlidingWindow[In, Acc, Out](agg: GroupAggregator[In, Acc, Out], size: Int, total: Acc, buffer: List[Acc]):
  def push(in: In): SlidingWindow[In, Acc, Out] =
    val prepared  = agg.prepare(in)
    val entered   = agg.group.combine(total, prepared)
    val extended  = buffer :+ prepared
    if extended.length > size then
      val evicted = agg.group.combine(entered, agg.group.inverse(extended.head))
      SlidingWindow(agg, size, evicted, extended.tail)
    else
      SlidingWindow(agg, size, entered, extended)
  def current: Out = agg.present(total)

def emptyWindow[In, Acc, Out](agg: GroupAggregator[In, Acc, Out], size: Int): SlidingWindow[In, Acc, Out] =
  SlidingWindow(agg, size, agg.group.empty, List())
```

`xs.foldLeft(emptyWindow(SumGroupAgg(), 3))((w, x) => w.push(x)).current` folds the whole stream and
reads the final window's total; calling `.current` after each `.push` gives the running sliding sum
at every point, which is the actual point of a sliding window over a live stream.

## `groupBy` — no new primitive, `Map[K, Acc]` is a `Monoid` too

If `Acc` is a `Monoid`, `Map[K, Acc]` is a `Monoid`: combine two maps key-by-key, using `Acc`'s
`combine` on keys present in both, passing through keys present in only one. "Group by key,
aggregate per group" is this `Map`-monoid wrapping any `Aggregator`'s own monoid — not a separate
concept the language needs. Tuples are destructured with a `val` binding inside the lambda body
(`val (k, v) = kv`), not as lambda parameters, matching this module's own §5.2 finding about a
tuple-destructuring lambda parameter miscompiling on the reference front. `Map[K, V]()` builds the
empty map, not `Map.empty` — see `v1/runtime/backend/interpreter/BUGS.md`
`map-dot-empty-reads-empty-as-a-literal-key-not-the-companion-accessor`:

```scalascript
class MapMonoid[K, Acc](inner: Monoid[Acc]) extends Monoid[Map[K, Acc]]:
  def empty: Map[K, Acc] = Map[K, Acc]()
  def combine(a: Map[K, Acc], b: Map[K, Acc]): Map[K, Acc] =
    b.toList.foldLeft(a) { (acc, kv) =>
      val (k, v) = kv
      acc.get(k) match
        case Some(existing) => acc.updated(k, inner.combine(existing, v))
        case None           => acc.updated(k, v)
    }

def groupByAgg[K, In, Acc, Out](xs: List[(K, In)], agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]): Map[K, Out] =
  val monoid = MapMonoid[K, Acc](agg.monoid)
  val acc = xs.foldLeft(monoid.empty) { (m, kv) =>
    val (k, item) = kv
    monoid.combine(m, Map(k -> agg.prepare(item)))
  }
  acc.toList.foldLeft(Map[K, Out]()) { (m, kv) =>
    val (k, v) = kv
    m.updated(k, agg.present(v))
  }
```

`groupByAgg` runs any `Aggregator` per key in one pass — e.g. `groupByAgg(sales.map(s => (s.region,
s.amount)), SumAgg())` gives total sales per region. This is exactly what `keyBy` + `aggregatePerKey`
already do on `DStream` (see `specs/distributed-streams.md`); this is the same algebraic fact,
usable directly over an in-memory `List`.

## Bridge to `DStream`/`Pipeline` and `Dataset`

`specs/distributed-streams.md` §5.2/§5.8 already defines the raw primitives an `Aggregator` slots
into directly — `aggregatePerKey(z)(f)(g)`/`runFold(z)(f)`/`combinePerKey(f)`, with real backends
(native, Spark, Kafka Streams, Flink, Beam) already implemented. Two small helpers make the bridge
mechanical rather than something every caller hand-writes:

```scalascript
def aggregatorSeqOp[In, Acc, Out](agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]): (Acc, In) => Acc =
  (acc, in) => agg.monoid.combine(acc, agg.prepare(in))

def aggregatorCombOp[In, Acc, Out](agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out]): (Acc, Acc) => Acc =
  (a, b) => agg.monoid.combine(a, b)

// stream.keyBy(keyFn).aggregatePerKey(agg.monoid.empty)(aggregatorSeqOp(agg))(aggregatorCombOp(agg))
```

Verified standalone (without a live `DStream` backend, which needs plugin infrastructure beyond
`std/aggregator.ssc` itself): `aggregatorSeqOp`/`aggregatorCombOp` are what `aggregatePerKey` calls
internally, so folding two "partitions" with `seqOp` and merging their results with `combOp` must
equal folding the whole input with `seqOp` directly — exactly the property that licenses running
`aggregatePerKey` across any number of real partitions:

```scalascript
val agg    = CountAgg[String]()
val seqOp  = aggregatorSeqOp(agg)
val combOp = aggregatorCombOp(agg)
val part1  = List("a", "b", "c").foldLeft(agg.monoid.empty)(seqOp)
val part2  = List("d", "e").foldLeft(agg.monoid.empty)(seqOp)
combOp(part1, part2)   // => 5, same as counting all five elements in one partition
```

This is a proof of the bridge's mechanism, not an end-to-end `DStream` integration test — it does
not exercise `aggregatePerKey` itself, windowing, or any backend; that stays real, separate work
(see `BACKLOG.md`) if a caller wants it verified live rather than by this equivalence.

## Rendering — a shape, not a new primitive

An `Aggregator`'s `Out`, or a `groupByAgg` result's `Map[K, Out]`, becomes renderable the moment it
turns into `List[String]` headers and `List[List[String]]` rows — `std/ui/data.ssc`'s live table
and `examples/std-ui/table.ssc`'s static one already consume exactly that shape, and `jsonStringify`
(`std/json.ssc`) already turns a `Map`/`List` into JSON directly, no bridge needed. `mapToRows` is
the one piece actually missing — turning a `groupByAgg` result into that row shape. String
interpolation (`s"$k"`), not `.toString`, is what stays consistent across lanes: a `Double` like
`13.0` renders as `"13"` via `s"$v"` on every lane, but `.toString` gives `"13.0"` specifically on
`run-jvm` (real Scala's own formatting) while every other lane still shows `"13"` — found running
this conformance case for real, not assumed:

```scalascript
def mapToRows[K, V](m: Map[K, V]): List[List[String]] =
  m.toList.map { kv =>
    val (k, v) = kv
    List(s"$k", s"$v")
  }

def renderTableHtml(headers: List[String], rows: List[List[String]]): String =
  val head = headers.map(h => "<th>" + h + "</th>").mkString
  val body = rows.map(row => "<tr>" + row.map(v => "<td>" + v + "</td>").mkString + "</tr>").mkString
  "<table><thead><tr>" + head + "</tr></thead><tbody>" + body + "</tbody></table>"
```

`renderTableHtml` itself is not `Aggregator`-specific (any `headers`/`rows` pair works) — it exists
here only as a standalone, dependency-free way to verify the shape `mapToRows` produces is actually
consumable, without requiring `std/ui/data.ssc`'s live-table machinery just to check a string.
Charts remain the genuine gap (§10.3 of the spec) — no new mechanism is needed once one exists, but
building it is real, separate work.

## Effects — the base shape, not the composition

`Aggregator.prepare`/`present` are total, pure functions; a real record's `prepare` may need to
validate, parse-and-possibly-fail, or enrich from an effect. `EffAggregator` is the same three-part
shape with `prepare`/`present` returning `F[_]` instead of a bare value — `monoid` stays pure, since
merging accumulated state has no reason to become effectful just because producing one did:

```scalascript
trait EffAggregator[F[_], In, Acc, Out]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Acc]
  def prepare(in: In): F[Acc]
  def present(acc: Acc): F[Out]

def runEffAggregator[F[_], In, Acc, Out](
    xs: List[In], agg: EffAggregator[F, In, Acc, Out], m: Monad[F]
): F[Out] =
  val accF: F[Acc] = xs.foldLeft(m.pure(agg.monoid.empty)) { (accF, in) =>
    accF.flatMap(acc => agg.prepare(in).flatMap(prepared => m.pure(agg.monoid.combine(acc, prepared))))
  }
  accF.flatMap(agg.present)
```

Verified with `F = Option`, a `prepare` that fails the whole aggregation on a negative input:

```scalascript
class ValidatingSum() extends EffAggregator[Option, Int, Int, Int]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Int] = intSum
  def prepare(in: Int): Option[Int] = if in >= 0 then Some(in) else None
  def present(acc: Int): Option[Int] = Some(acc)

runEffAggregator(List(1, 2, 3), ValidatingSum(), optionMonad)    // => Some(6)
runEffAggregator(List(1, -2, 3), ValidatingSum(), optionMonad)   // => None
```

**`LiftAgg` (lifting a pure `Aggregator` into `EffAggregator`) and `ZipEffAgg` (composing two
effectful aggregators via `Applicative.ap`) are NOT shipped here**, despite being fully specified —
both are ordinary classes parameterized by a higher-kinded `F[_]`, holding a constructor field whose
type also involves `F` (`Applicative[F]` for the former, an `EffAggregator[F, ...]` field for the
latter), and BOTH throw a bogus `arity: N expected, M given` at runtime the moment this module's own
`VarianceAcc` (§5.1, needs its `val` fields for the JVM lane) is anywhere in the same program's
import graph — confirmed via a minimal, fully standalone repro unrelated to aggregators at all. See
`v2/BUGS.md` `higher-kinded-generic-field-corrupts-arity-of-an-unrelated-val-class-elsewhere-in-scope`
and `BACKLOG.md` for what a fix (or a working design that avoids the pattern) would need.

`ContramapAgg`/`dimapAgg` (§11.3's profunctor shape) are unaffected — `Aggregator` itself is not
higher-kinded, so neither hits the pattern above:

```scalascript
class ContramapAgg[In2, In, Acc, Out](agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out], f: In2 => In)
    extends Aggregator[In2, Acc, Out]:
  def monoid: Monoid[Acc] = agg.monoid
  def prepare(in: In2): Acc = agg.prepare(f(in))
  def present(acc: Acc): Out = agg.present(acc)

def dimapAgg[In2, In, Acc, Out, Out2](
    agg: Aggregator[In, Acc, Out], f: In2 => In, g: Out => Out2
): Aggregator[In2, Acc, Out2] =
  MapAgg(ContramapAgg(agg, f), g)
```

Verified: adapting `SumAgg` to sum STRING LENGTHS instead — `ContramapAgg(SumAgg(), (s: String) =>
s.length.toDouble)` run over `List("a", "bb", "ccc")` gives `6`; `dimapAgg` additionally formatting
the output gives `"total=6"`. The formal `Profunctor[P[_, _]]` typeclass instance for `Aggregator`
(spec §11.3) is not shipped as library code either — it was verified working via a standalone
script earlier in this project's history (`v2/BUGS.md`
`given-extension-typehead-mismatch-silently-returns-receiver`, fixed 2026-08-29), but no
`std/profunctor.ssc` module exists yet to hold the formal `Profunctor` trait itself; `ContramapAgg`/
`dimapAgg` above are, per the spec's own framing, not less "correct" for being ordinary functions.

Arrows, `Category`, and a new `Future`/`Task`/`Result` value type stay deliberately out of scope —
§11.4 of the spec gives the reason and it still holds.
